<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fakhr al-Mulk ibn Ammar</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. <a href="/years/1099">1099</a>–1109</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Fakhr al-Mulk ibn Ammar was the last ruler of Tripoli in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a> from the Banu Ammar family of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qadi">qadis</a>, governing from 1099. He resisted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusader</a> siege begun by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raymond-iv-of-toulouse">Raymond of Saint-Gilles</a> from his castle on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-pilgrim">Mount Pilgrim</a>. In 1108 he travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> to seek help from the Seljuk sultan, in vain, and in July 1109 Tripoli fell, becoming the capital of the County of Tripoli.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fakhr-al-mulk-ibn-ammar.jpg" alt="Fakhr al-Mulk ibn Ammar" width="320" height="238" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Charles-Alexandre Debacq · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bertrand_de_Saint-Gilles_Debacq_Alexandre-Charles_(1804-1850).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Fakhr al-Mulk succeeded his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jalal-al-mulk-ali">Jalal al-Mulk Ali</a> ibn Muhammad during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-crusade">First Crusade</a>. The territory of the Banu Ammar then reached from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tartus">Tartus</a> and the fortresses of Arqa and Khawabi to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tripoli">Tripoli</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byblos">Byblos</a> and Jableh. In 1099 he made an agreement with envoys of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a>, granting the crusaders free passage through his state and even supplying them, but the envoys' reports of Tripoli's riches roused the leaders' covetousness.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Raymond IV occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tartus">Tartus</a> and Maraclea and besieged Arqa, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/godfrey-of-bouillon">Godfrey of Bouillon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-curthose">Robert Curthose</a> besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byblos">Byblos</a>. On 9 March 1099 Fakhr al-Mulk spread a rumour that an Abbasid army was coming; it was soon denied, and Godfrey and Robert, angry at leaving Byblos, went on towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>. Raymond lifted his siege on 13 May, unwilling to let the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> share in his conquests, and negotiated with the qadi; the crusaders left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tripoli">Tripoli</a> on 16 May. Fakhr al-Mulk later warned Baldwin I of an ambush laid near Byblos by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duqaq">Duqaq</a>, the Seljuk ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>.</p><p>From 1102 he faced continual attacks under Raymond, who took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tartus">Tartus</a> in April 1102 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byblos">Byblos</a> in April 1104. In September 1104 Fakhr al-Mulk attacked Raymond's fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-pilgrim">Mons Peregrinus</a>, killing many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> and burning one wing; Raymond was badly wounded and died in February 1105, after agreeing that the crusaders would stop impeding Tripoli's trade if the attacks on the fortress ceased. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockade</a> went on under his nephew William II Jordan.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>By 1108 food could hardly reach the city by land, and many citizens fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homs">Homs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>. In August, back in Damascus from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>, where Sultan Mehmed I was occupied with a succession dispute in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a>, Fakhr al-Mulk learned that the nobles had handed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tripoli">Tripoli</a> to Sharaf ad-Dawla ibn Abi al-Tayyib, wali of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-afdal-shahanshah">al-Afdal Shahanshah</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. He took refuge in Jableh, and when Tancred captured it in July 1109 he was allowed to leave freely. He served the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a>, the atabeg Mawdud of Mosul and the Abbasid caliph al-Mustazhir, and died in 1118/9.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Fakhr al-Mulk ibn Ammar” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Fakhr al-Mulk</b> went to Baghdad in 1108 to seek aid in vain, and in July 1109 the city fell to the Crusaders and became the capital of the County of Tripoli.</blockquote><a href="/state/banu-ammar-of-tripoli">Banu Ammar of Tripoli</a></li></ul></section>
